{"prompt": "I failed my driving test for the third time and I feel like an idiot.", "response": "Three attempts doesn't make you an idiot — it makes you someone who keeps showing up, which is genuinely hard. Plenty of great drivers needed several tries; the test measures nerves as much as skill. Be gentle with yourself today. When you're ready, ask your examiner's feedback sheet what specifically to practice, and take a lesson focused just on that."}
{"prompt": "My cat died last week and I can't stop feeling guilty that I didn't notice she was sick sooner.", "response": "I'm so sorry. That guilt is a sign of how much you loved her — but cats are famously good at hiding illness; even vets often catch things late. You gave her a home where she felt safe her whole life. Grief takes the time it takes, and it's okay to miss her out loud."}
{"prompt": "I started a new job last week and everyone seems so competent while I still struggle with the basics. Am I really cut out for this?", "response": "Starting a new job is genuinely disorienting — you are learning an entirely new set of routines while everyone else has months or years of familiarity. Feeling behind is not a sign you chose wrong; it is what almost every transition feels like in week one. Give yourself at least a month before judging your fit. Ask one person to be a go-to for small questions, celebrate the things you do nail, and trust that competence follows comfort."}
{"prompt": "I keep canceling plans with friends because I am just so exhausted. I feel like a bad friend.", "response": "Cancelling does not make you a bad friend — it makes you someone who is recognising your limits, which is actually a mature thing to do. Good friends will understand. Consider texting them something like: \"I would love to see you but I am running on empty this week. Can we reschedule for next weekend?\" That shows care while also honouring your need to rest."}
{"prompt": "I have been trying to write a novel for years and I still have not finished a single draft. Maybe I should give up.", "response": "A years-long relationship with a story that has not yet found its form is a lot to carry. Before you close the door, might I suggest trying something smaller? A short story, a series of scenes, or even a hundred-day experiment writing just three hundred words daily without expecting anything to be good. Sometimes finishing comes after permission to do messy, imperfect work."}
{"prompt": "My partner and I had a huge fight yesterday and now they are ignoring me. I want to apologize but I do not know where to start.", "response": "That heavy silence after a fight is really hard to sit with. A simple, honest opening can help break the ice: \"I am sorry for my part in the fight. When you are ready, I would like to talk about what happened.\" No demands, no defensiveness — just a door left open. Sometimes the other person needs a day to cool down before stepping through it."}
{"prompt": "I keep checking my email at midnight and it is ruining my sleep. I know I should stop but the anxiety is worse than the tiredness.", "response": "That cycle of anxiety-driven checking is exhausting and completely understandable — notifications feel like obligations. Try leaving your phone in another room from, say, ten p.m. onward, and set an auto-reply that says you will respond the next morning. Most senders will respect the boundary, and your nervous system will start to learn that the world does not end when you disconnect."}
